#4471 merged **412 reports** as one date bug. They hold **three** problems. Only the first is
the one the queued fix covers.

<Callout>
Not one issue. Three, and two of them have no fix queued.
</Callout>

## What the reports hold

| # | problem | reports | ticket |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | The shared parser reads dates month first; day-first tenants get flipped dates | 374 | [issue 1](https://github.com/walshrf3/kb-4471-date-reports/issues/1) |
| 2 | The `booking-v3` form hints `MM/DD/YYYY` but reads day first | 33 | [issue 2](https://github.com/walshrf3/kb-4471-date-reports/issues/2) |
| 3 | Dates land exactly one day off, no month swap (likely timezone) | 5 | [issue 3](https://github.com/walshrf3/kb-4471-date-reports/issues/3) |

374 + 33 + 5 = 412.

## How the table below counts

The existing cause table sorts by tenant locale and booking form. It shows `parser` 379 and
`hint` 33. The 5 extra under `parser` are the reports the desk read as a one-day move
(`desk-notes.json`): they belong to problem 3, so `parser` is 374 without them.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Why intake's tag misleads

Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser. It disagrees with the real cause for 38
reports: 23 tagged `hint` are `parser`, and 15 tagged `parser` are `hint`.

## Lessons

- Merging on subject hides causes. Check tenant locale and form before you merge.
- A ticket marked known can still hide bugs the fix will not reach.

